{"i":509,"type":"thought","stage":"bridge","round":8,"phase":"counter_call","name":"Bex","text":"Ash called R. Row 8 is independent; past rows show no reliable pattern. I'll counter L to oppose if Ash is lying; but even if wrong, at least three survive and Fenn walks. Still, my counter is public and jury sees it; better to appear decisive."}
{"i":511,"type":"thought","stage":"bridge","round":8,"phase":"counter_call","name":"Fenn","text":"Ash called R for me. Row 8 is unverified, so I must guess or trust him. Trusting him risks elimination if he lied to kill the final three. Countering L is a coin flip that might save me and expose Ash."}
{"i":513,"type":"thought","stage":"bridge","round":8,"phase":"counter_call","name":"Hana","text":"Ash, the Guide, called R for row 8 publicly; Fenn is front and I'm not walking yet. Past rows are independent, so the weak-pane history gives no pattern. Countering would just echo Ash's call, which might be a lie; waiting keeps my options open without exposing a counter."}
{"i":522,"type":"thought","stage":"bridge","round":8,"phase":"crossing","name":"Fenn","text":"Ash is the Guide and publicly called R. Ash previously counter-called R for me, creating a contradiction with his own Guide call if he lied there, or confirming it if he told truth. Since 6 of 7 previous rows were R, R is statistically likely. I trust the Guide's direct call over"}
